NoteAI Generated Code





module led_blink #(
    parameter int unsigned CLK_FREQ_HZ = 48_000_000,
    parameter int unsigned BLINK_HZ    = 2
) (
    output logic led
);

    // Toggle the LED every half-period.
    localparam int unsigned HALF_PERIOD =
        CLK_FREQ_HZ / (2 * BLINK_HZ);

    // Width automatically sized for the required count.
    localparam int unsigned COUNTER_WIDTH =
        (HALF_PERIOD &lt;= 1) ? 1 : $clog2(HALF_PERIOD);

    logic clk;
    logic [COUNTER_WIDTH-1:0] counter;

    // Lattice iCE40 UltraPlus internal high-frequency oscillator.
    HSOSC #(
        .CLKHF_DIV(2'b00)       // 48 MHz
    ) hfosc (
        .CLKHFPU(1'b1),          // Power up oscillator
        .CLKHFEN(1'b1),          // Enable clock output
        .CLKHF  (clk)
    );

    // Divide 48 MHz down to a 2 Hz LED blink.
    always_ff @(posedge clk) begin
        if (counter == HALF_PERIOD - 1) begin
            counter &lt;= '0;
            led     &lt;= ~led;
        end
        else begin
            counter &lt;= counter + 1'b1;
        end
    end

endmodule



I used the free non-signed-in version of ChatGPT to generate this code using the provided LLM prompt. Initially the code did not compile due to it assuming that I was using “Yosys/nexpnr”, which lead it to use SB_HFOSC instead of HSOSC. However, after I specified that I was using Lattice Radiant, it switched it to using the correct HSOSC module. This was able to successfully synthesize and implement on the FPGA, and the LED blinked at the correct frequency.
I would say that is code is of fairly decent quality. It comments well, uses descriptive variable names, and uses cohesive capitalization and formatting. However, it over defines many variables, and seems to be overcomplicated for the task at hand. It used some things that I had never seen before, such as using underscores as commas to make large numbers more readable, localparams, and the $clog2 function. If I were to use an LLM in my workflow, I would give it clear instructions of how I want it to achieve the task. This way the LLM will not overcomplicate the code and will act as a helper that can write the easy parts of the code while I focus on the more difficult parts.

Design and Testing Methodology
First, the design for multiplexing the seven segment display was made. This functioned by taking the inputs from 8 switches, and selecting between 4 of them based on the state of a counter. This counter had a flag at 96Hz, so each switch signal would be read once every 96Hz. This was sent to a decoder which choose which segments of a display to activate. That signal and the two flags were outputted: one flag would power one side of the seven segment display, and the other flag would power the other side. This approach was able to minimize the number of IO pins used and minimize the power drawn.






NoteMultiplexer Implementation





    //Defining a multiplexer cycle to be at 96Hz
    Counter #(.WIDTH(18), .MAX(18'd250000)) multiplexerCount(
        .clk(clk),
        .reset(reset),
        .enable(1'b1),
        .blink(multBlinkA)
        );

    logic [3:0] tempSwitches;

    //As the cycle is 96Hz, each display is powered for 96 times a second, preventing blinking
    assign tempSwitches = multBlinkA? s[3:0] : s[7:4]; //When high, the right four switches are used, when low, the left four switches are used
    //assign seg = tempSeg; //When high, the display of lower signficance is used, when low, the last display of higher significance is used
    assign multBlinkB = ~(multBlinkA);
    sevenSeg segments(
        .bitsIn(tempSwitches),
        .diodeSeg(seg)
        );




Next, the keypad scanner was designed. This used a counter that overflowed at 2Hz. Based on the current state of the counter, different pins would be activate. Therefore, the scanner would iterate through powering each row of the keypad, completing one full cycle every 2Hz.






NoteKeypad Scanner Implementation





    logic [24:0] counter;
    Counter #(.WIDTH(25), .MAX(25'd24000000)) scannerCount(
        .clk(clk),
        .reset(reset),
        .enable(enable),
        .counter(counter)
        );

    assign rows = ((counter &lt;= 25'd6000000)&& reset)? 4'b1000:  //As the scanner blinks, it will alternate between powering each row
                  ((counter &lt;= 25'd12000000) && reset)? 4'b0100:   //As the scanner swaps twice per scannerBlink cycle, it will swap rows at 8Hz
                  ((counter &lt;= 25'd18000000) && reset)? 4'b0010:  //So it will complete a full cycle every 1/2 second, or 2Hz
                  ((counter &lt;= 25'd24000000) && reset)? 4'b0001:
                  (~reset)? 4'b1000 : 4'b1000;                                   //At reset, set to starting state




Following this, the output columns of the keypad would be taken as the inputs. Based on which buttons were pressed, various columns of the keypad would be shorted to the rows. Based on the columns that were powered, various LEDs would be activated. Depending on which buttons were pressed, this led to a “cycling” appearance as the rows were not constantly powered.
Lastly, the verilog implementation was tested using top level and module level testbenches.

Design and Testing Methodology
For the delays, a timer was set to overflow at a rate of 1 KHz as the length of all inputs was in ms. As the onboard MSI generated a signal at 4 MHz, it was divided using TIM6 down to 1 KHz. This was tested using an oscilloscope to confirm that notes lasted as long as they were described in code.






NoteDelay Code





void msDelay(int ms, bool rest) {
    while(--ms &gt;= 0) {
        //will be high once every ms
        while (!(TIM6-&gt;SR)){
            //only pin when it is not a rest
            if((TIM7-&gt;SR) & (!rest)){
                togglePin(OUT_PIN);
                //reset flag as it needs to be done manually
                TIM7-&gt;SR &= (0);
            }
        }
        //reset flag as it needs to be done manually
        TIM6-&gt;SR &= (0);  
   }
}



For the notes themselves, a similar structure of using a timer to divide the 4MHz MSI clock was used. For this, TIM7 was used. To prevent dividing by 0, the code first checked that the frequency of a note was not 0 before calculating the counter needed to achieve this frequency. For both the delay and the frequency, the status register was used to evaluate when the counter had overflowed. This was tested by setting a long duration note to a known frequency, and using an oscilloscope to confirm that the outputted frequency matched.






NoteNote Code





bool setFreq(int freq){
    //Frequency 0 means the note is rest
    if(freq == 0){
        //don't output power during a rest
        digitalWrite(OUT_PIN, 0);
        return true;
    }else{
        //See calculations for math
        uint16_t counter = CLOCK_FREQUENCY / (2*freq);
        TIM7-&gt;ARR = counter-1;
        //Counter could be greater than ARR, and it only roles over when "counter reaches the overflow [ARR]"
        TIM7-&gt;CNT = 0;
        //Flag could become high during a rest and wouldnot be reset
        TIM7-&gt;SR &= (0);
        return false;
    }
}



The electronics were designed according to the schematics shown in the amplifiers datasheet. However, a ~10x voltage divider (see calculations) was implemented around a 10 K ohm potentiometer to gain more accuracy in volume control. The electronics were tested using a 8 ohm resistor to prevent overpowering the speaker.
Lastly, the overall design was tested in practice by using a speaker to confirm everything sounded accurate.

Design and Testing Methodology
First, the SPI communication system was designed. This was done by using CMSIS headers to enable the required pins. Following this, a base communication block was designed. This block would wait for any proceeding messages to end, send a 1 byte message, then record the resulting message.






NoteSPI Send Receive





char spiSendReceive(char send){
    while (!(SPI1-&gt;SR & SPI_SR_TXE));
    *((volatile char *) (&SPI1-&gt;DR)) = send;
    while (!(SPI1-&gt;SR & SPI_SR_RXNE));
    return *((volatile char *) (&SPI1-&gt;DR));
}



After that is completed, the block was designed to be ran twice to actually create the fundamental messages that are used in an SPI connection.






NoteSPI Messaging Blocks





void spiWrite(char address, char write){
    digitalWrite(CE_PIN, 1);
    spiSendReceive(address);
    spiSendReceive(write); 
    while (SPI1-&gt;SR & SPI_SR_BSY);
    digitalWrite(CE_PIN, 0);
}

char spiRead(char address){
    digitalWrite(CE_PIN, 1);
    spiSendReceive(address);
    char val = spiSendReceive(0x00); //empty as this is just to receive data.
    while (SPI1-&gt;SR & SPI_SR_BSY);
    digitalWrite(CE_PIN, 0);
    return val;
}



To test this, a basic SPI communication block was ran, and each of the four signals used in an SPI connection (CE/EN, SCK/SCLK, MOSI/SDI, MISO/SDO) were measured on an oscilloscope to ensure that the communication was ran properly.






Figure 1: Waveform of an SPI signalling block.



Each of these communication blocks were used in the main loop. This loop would wait for a user input to be sent into the web page, which would get passed over UART from the ESP to the MCU. Then, the MCU would read the input, and based on it either change the LED state or send the new temperature resolution to the DS1722. Following this, the current temperature would be read, and it and the current LED state would be written to HTML code. This HTML code would then be sent back to the ESP to be broadcasted on the website.






NoteMain Code Loop





  while(1) {
